Here, the ItemService class uses dependency injection to inject a new
ConnectionFactory instance and the EventBusSettings into the class. As you may
have noticed, the DeleteItemAsync method also calls the SendDeleteMessage we
defined. Furthermore, the SendDeleteMessage method uses the CreateConnection
method in order to create a new connection with RabbitMQ. Then, it continues by creating
a new fresh channel using the CreateModel method and defining a new queue that has the
same name as the queue we defined in the EventBusSettings configuration. Finally, it
proceeds by serializing the ItemSoldOut event and publishing the message using the
EventQueue field. The whole procedure is wrapped into a try-catch block in order to be
ignored in case there is a communication error between the catalog service and the queue.
